<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN"
          "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <!--
    Entitlements for the hardened runtime, which Developer ID signing requires
    and notarization refuses to proceed without.

    Deliberately almost empty. qub is not sandboxed, so the entitlements that
    open up network access, file access and the keychain are not needed here —
    those gate the App Sandbox, and outside it the app already has them. Every
    dylib and plugin in the bundle is signed with the same identity as the app,
    so library validation has nothing to complain about either.

    allow-jit is the exception. The QML engine compiles hot bindings at runtime
    and asks the kernel for MAP_JIT memory to do it; under the hardened runtime
    that request is denied without this entitlement, and the app dies on the
    generated code rather than on startup. It is the narrow form: it does not
    permit unsigned executable memory in general, only pages the app maps
    through the JIT interface. If a build is ever produced with the QML JIT
    compiled out, this can go.
  -->
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
  <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
